  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/7A213E">Definition <span data-tag="7A213E">8.3.1.1</span>.</a>Quillen bifunctorleft Quillen bifunctor Let $\mathbf{A}$, $\mathbf{B}$, and $\mathbf{C}$ be model categories. We will say that a functor $F: \mathbf{A}\times \mathbf{B}\rightarrow \mathbf{C}$ is a <em>left Quillen bifunctor</em> if the following conditions are satisfied: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(a)$"><p>
Let $i: A \rightarrow A'$ and $j: B \rightarrow B'$ be cofibrations in $\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{B}$, respectively. Then the induced map 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ i \wedge j: F(A',B) \coprod _{F(A,B)} F(A,B') \rightarrow F(A',B') \]
</div>
<p>
 is a cofibration in $\mathbf{C}$. Moreover, if either $i$ or $j$ is a trivial cofibration, then $i \wedge j$ is also a trivial cofibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(b)$"><p>
The functor $F$ preserves small colimits separately in each variable. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
